{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1123 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3a66ed6294724f9c0442647aa40fe7f006c8334c47737a0f9107c7d5dd9f256c" :edb/db-before-t 1122 :edb/db-after-t 1123 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534435 50 #inst "2026-10-07T03:42:11.356Z" 13194139534435 true] [17592186078405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288063 13194139534435 true] [17592186096535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288098 13194139534435 true] [17592186105830 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288115 13194139534435 true] [17592186138425 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288108 13194139534435 true] [17592186152042 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288070 13194139534435 true] [17592186179590 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288084 13194139534435 true] [17592186180119 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534435 true] [17592186180119 1100 "Write $4 + \\frac{4}{n}$ as a single fraction." 13194139534435 false] [17592186180119 1100 "Write $4+\\frac{4}{n}$ as a single fraction." 13194139534435 true] [17592186180119 1145 "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $n$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n+4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(n+1)}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186184092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288077 13194139534435 true] [17592186184264 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288101 13194139534435 true] [17592186204559 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534435 true] [17592186204559 1100 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y = 3\\sin (2x + \\frac{π}{2})$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534435 false] [17592186204559 1100 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534435 true] [17592186204559 1145 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is any integer." 13194139534435 true] [17592186217101 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288091 13194139534435 true] [17592186288063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288064 13194139534435 true] [17592186288063 1089 "q-105396" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288063 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534435 true] [17592186288063 1100 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-3,1]?$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288063 1113 #uuid "f594b9d5-1b7c-5654-b394-ec6bdd7e9748" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288063 1145 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $g(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-3,1).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-3,1].$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $h(x)$, $p(x)$, and $q(x)$ are not continuous at some point that lies inside the interval $(-3,1].$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288065 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288066 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288067 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288068 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288069 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1041 "selection" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288066 13194139534435 true] [17592186288064 1110 #uuid "35e64751-a331-54a5-825b-fabe7c00029b" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288065 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/198f84781bcafa5560244ccf6e87df23a8d3e0ab621aaedb3c17df3fe1e52e31.png" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534435 true] [17592186288065 1111 #uuid "ecdaf760-2968-51ea-921d-5893dbb0fbb0" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288066 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534435 true] [17592186288066 1111 #uuid "76ad1157-63d4-5359-84d7-4393d90a4dfb" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288067 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/6d66f5a7373826c316010328eaab1d6ac4cf64a82f9c7038b1f6f9450efd3388.png" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534435 true] [17592186288067 1111 #uuid "b1b90f28-5ea5-5270-ab42-0dc5760c1dde" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288068 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/4df80bb9377923dfd59a686761c6dc3bd7517213f4da3f0e39401215ea22dbdd.png" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534435 true] [17592186288068 1111 #uuid "74f7b97e-896c-538c-b6a5-236b52d53497" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288069 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/7c1114ff20331e5a1100523a460b8dddb017ea54389deb5f9388d16da4742d9e.png" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534435 true] [17592186288069 1111 #uuid "b59db92c-e7bf-5c11-9227-1d9d4adf9ed8" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288071 13194139534435 true] [17592186288070 1089 "q-112230" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534435 true] [17592186288070 1100 "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $5(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},$ $\\,6(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},$ and $12(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2}.$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288070 1113 #uuid "08133ea2-e4bf-5c08-8db2-67322f4e831a" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288070 1145 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(5,6,12):$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,…,\\,55,\\,60,\\,65,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,\\,12,…,\\,54,\\,60,\\,66,…$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(5,6,12)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},$ and $(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2},$ we find the largest powers for $(x-5)$ and $(y-2)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x-5)^{5},(x-5)^{3},$ and $(x-5)^{3},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\n- For $(y-2)^{3},(y-2)^{3},$ and $(y-2)^{2},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x-5)$ and $(y-2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},\\,(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},\\,(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2})=(x-5)^{5}\\cdot (y-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(5(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},\\,6(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},\\,12(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2}) & = \\\\ 60\\cdot (x-5)^{5}\\cdot (y-2)^{3} & = \\\\ 60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288072 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288073 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288074 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288075 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288076 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1041 "selection" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288072 13194139534435 true] [17592186288071 1110 #uuid "dd860098-0027-5c39-a5a3-2808bdcc7a1e" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288072 1034 "60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288072 1111 #uuid "a8aed979-6828-5452-8c47-f9b99509f70d" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288073 1034 "90(x-5)^{11}(y-2)^{7}" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288073 1111 #uuid "ef3d70c5-345b-5d27-834c-a78acffee3a6" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288074 1034 "90(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288074 1111 #uuid "11ec50ed-3e27-55c9-ad05-62b94a27e393" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288075 1034 "30(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288075 1111 #uuid "b74ccb9b-cd4b-57a5-a4a0-33fb501e7699" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288076 1034 "60(x-5)^{11}(y-2)^{7}" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288076 1111 #uuid "778f6a04-cba3-5d0a-b75c-5ab69dca105b" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288078 13194139534435 true] [17592186288077 1089 "q-117799" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534435 true] [17592186288077 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/c9ed24a7fa07d6c29e4125c8428fb61a42fc969b99f101c4737bdedf9bf036ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288077 1113 #uuid "554b2923-f03c-51dd-9817-6e0a2f8280b5" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288077 1145 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{4}+(-3)=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})-3.\n$$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288079 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288080 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288081 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288082 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288083 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1041 "selection" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288081 13194139534435 true] [17592186288078 1110 #uuid "0a804024-353d-51a6-ab20-6177b191b4a8" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288079 1034 "-\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288079 1111 #uuid "05ce52e6-f89f-553b-8e24-7079cde0acad" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288080 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288080 1111 #uuid "abc2b893-33c8-5d6f-b541-3795156fd5f6" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288081 1034 "-\\frac{9}{4}" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288081 1111 #uuid "a808053c-0a2e-5749-9579-6f15d0ed6659" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288082 1034 "2" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288082 1111 #uuid "877763b4-7bb3-595b-9419-5d46fc89bdc9" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288083 1034 "-1" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534435 true] [17592186288083 1111 #uuid "f9bc7e49-2330-59ab-afb3-ddb72b27b2ba" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288084 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288085 13194139534435 true] [17592186288084 1089 "q-19847" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288084 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534435 true] [17592186288084 1100 "Given that $n\\ge 0$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n!)^{2}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}.$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288084 1113 #uuid "5bb56c3d-ec97-553c-8bea-98b6d05242cd" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288084 1145 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(n+1)!=(n+1)n!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $n!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n!)^{2}}{((n+1)!)^{2}} & =\\frac{n!\\cdot n!}{(n+1)!\\cdot (n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{n!}\\cdot \\cancel{n!}}{(n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534435 true] [17592186288085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288086 13194139534435 true] [17592186288085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288087 13194139534435 true] [17592186288085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288088 13194139534435 true] [17592186288085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288089 13194139534435 true] [17592186288085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288090 13194139534435 true] [17592186288085 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534435 true] [17592186288085 1041 